    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-10567",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities",
      "problem": "$\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =3x-2\\pi ,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7[1+{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi )])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7{csc}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+7(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))+C \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
