    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-113833",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f4b340f6-0ac6-5bbf-a7d4-0088ca3c4e5e",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int {e}^{x/2\\,-\\,1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{2}-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{x/2\\,-\\,1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =2\\,{e}^{x/2\\,-\\,1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
