  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-14787",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/3e511986dc068a34c72397bed12b81b94dd2150cfba24efe8245318b42f23fdd.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$, the line segment for $x\\in [0,2]$ and the two line segments for $x\\in [2,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-5.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{9}{2}-5-7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
